WEBVTT

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:01.301
Welcome!

00:00:01.301 --> 00:00:04.196
In this Java tutorial, you'll throw custom

00:00:04.196 --> 00:00:07.090
exceptions to report application failures,

00:00:07.090 --> 00:00:10.122
declare checked exceptions that callers must

00:00:10.122 --> 00:00:13.292
handle or pass onward, and preserve an earlier

00:00:13.292 --> 00:00:14.739
failure as the cause.

00:00:15.382 --> 00:00:18.820
These skills give other parts of an application

00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:22.330
a clear failure contract, so they can respond to

00:00:22.330 --> 00:00:25.621
invalid requests while keeping the diagnostic

00:00:25.621 --> 00:00:29.132
details developers need to investigate problems.

00:00:29.775 --> 00:00:33.180
Today we'll build a quantity reader for campus

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:34.142
supply staff.

00:00:34.142 --> 00:00:37.325
Each entry is text representing a requested

00:00:37.325 --> 00:00:38.509
number of items.

00:00:38.509 --> 00:00:42.062
Our rule accepts zero or a positive integer, and

00:00:42.062 --> 00:00:44.875
rejects words and negative quantities.

00:00:45.519 --> 00:00:49.033
The reader should return an accepted quantity or

00:00:49.033 --> 00:00:51.082
explain why an entry failed.

00:00:51.082 --> 00:00:54.449
When number conversion fails first, we'll keep

00:00:54.449 --> 00:00:57.158
that original exception alongside our

00:00:57.158 --> 00:00:58.621
application message.

00:00:58.621 --> 00:01:01.769
Each request will receive its own response.

00:01:02.436 --> 00:01:05.316
Now that we're in the Workspace, let's open

00:01:05.316 --> 00:01:05.986
Main.java.

00:01:09.732 --> 00:01:10.934
The file is open.

00:01:10.934 --> 00:01:14.186
Our caller needs one application-specific kind

00:01:14.186 --> 00:01:17.367
of failure to handle, whether the text cannot

00:01:17.367 --> 00:01:20.195
become a number or the number breaks our

00:01:20.195 --> 00:01:21.185
quantity rule.

00:01:21.185 --> 00:01:24.296
Let's define InvalidQuantityException with a

00:01:24.296 --> 00:01:26.982
message and an optional earlier cause.

00:01:41.738 --> 00:01:44.182
Extending Exception makes this a checked

00:01:44.182 --> 00:01:45.098
exception type.

00:01:45.098 --> 00:01:47.970
In ordinary Java method code, a checked failure

00:01:47.970 --> 00:01:50.597
that can escape must be caught or declared.

00:01:50.597 --> 00:01:53.407
The constructor forwards the message and cause

00:01:53.407 --> 00:01:54.996
to the parent constructor.

00:01:54.996 --> 00:01:57.867
Throwable is the common base type for throwable

00:01:57.867 --> 00:02:00.678
objects, so it can hold the earlier exception.

00:02:00.678 --> 00:02:03.488
A null cause means there is no earlier failure

00:02:03.488 --> 00:02:04.038
attached.

00:02:04.049 --> 00:02:07.186
Now we need the reader that either produces a

00:02:07.186 --> 00:02:10.045
quantity or reports that checked failure.

00:02:10.045 --> 00:02:13.182
Let's add QuantityParser and its read method,

00:02:13.182 --> 00:02:16.040
then declare the integer that will hold a

00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:17.574
successful conversion.

00:02:29.115 --> 00:02:32.245
The throws declaration belongs to the method

00:02:32.245 --> 00:02:32.743
header.

00:02:32.743 --> 00:02:36.016
It tells callers that InvalidQuantityException

00:02:36.016 --> 00:02:39.218
may escape; it does not raise an exception on

00:02:39.218 --> 00:02:40.000
every call.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:43.060
A successful call still returns an integer.

00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:46.332
That is our contract: a quantity on success, a

00:02:46.332 --> 00:02:48.040
named failure otherwise.

00:02:48.054 --> 00:02:51.091
First we have to discover whether the text

00:02:51.091 --> 00:02:52.682
represents an integer.

00:02:52.682 --> 00:02:56.153
Parsing means converting that text into a value.

00:02:56.153 --> 00:02:59.045
Let's try Integer.parseInt and catch the

00:02:59.045 --> 00:03:01.504
conversion failure it can produce.

00:03:11.191 --> 00:03:14.272
For text such as three, conversion supplies the

00:03:14.272 --> 00:03:15.190
integer three.

00:03:15.190 --> 00:03:18.206
A word such as two, written as letters, raises

00:03:18.206 --> 00:03:20.173
NumberFormatException instead.

00:03:20.173 --> 00:03:23.319
That is an unchecked exception, so Java does not

00:03:23.319 --> 00:03:25.876
require callers to catch or declare it.

00:03:25.876 --> 00:03:28.957
We catch it here because our application should

00:03:28.957 --> 00:03:31.908
report all invalid quantities through its own

00:03:31.908 --> 00:03:32.891
exception type.

00:03:32.908 --> 00:03:35.999
The conversion has already failed when this

00:03:35.999 --> 00:03:36.934
handler runs.

00:03:36.934 --> 00:03:39.882
Let's give that failure a useful quantity

00:03:39.882 --> 00:03:43.045
message while keeping the original exception

00:03:43.045 --> 00:03:44.483
object as its cause.

00:03:55.643 --> 00:03:58.796
Executing throw raises the new object and leaves

00:03:58.796 --> 00:04:01.423
this method without a returned quantity.

00:04:01.423 --> 00:04:04.182
The earlier NumberFormatException is still

00:04:04.182 --> 00:04:06.875
reachable through the new object's cause.

00:04:06.875 --> 00:04:09.568
We have added application context without

00:04:09.568 --> 00:04:11.867
discarding the original diagnostic.

00:04:11.867 --> 00:04:15.020
Since the method has left, the range check below

00:04:15.020 --> 00:04:16.793
will not run for that word.

00:04:16.814 --> 00:04:20.106
If conversion succeeds, we still need to check

00:04:20.106 --> 00:04:21.751
the application's rule.

00:04:21.751 --> 00:04:25.186
A negative integer is a number, but it is not an

00:04:25.186 --> 00:04:26.546
acceptable request.

00:04:26.546 --> 00:04:29.623
Let's reject negative quantities and return

00:04:29.623 --> 00:04:30.839
values that pass.

00:04:48.685 --> 00:04:51.723
This condition is validation: checking a value

00:04:51.723 --> 00:04:53.572
against an application rule.

00:04:53.572 --> 00:04:56.478
Negative one parses successfully, then fails

00:04:56.478 --> 00:04:57.799
this separate check.

00:04:57.799 --> 00:05:00.507
We pass null because there was no earlier

00:05:00.507 --> 00:05:02.555
conversion failure to preserve.

00:05:02.555 --> 00:05:05.196
Zero and positive integers reach return.

00:05:05.196 --> 00:05:08.301
The executed throw changes the path; the throws

00:05:08.301 --> 00:05:11.273
declaration describes that possibility to the

00:05:11.273 --> 00:05:11.735
caller.

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:13.172
Our reader is ready.

00:05:13.172 --> 00:05:16.068
Let's add Main, the class containing this

00:05:16.068 --> 00:05:19.316
standalone program, and main, the method where

00:05:19.316 --> 00:05:20.022
it starts.

00:05:20.022 --> 00:05:23.412
We'll prepare three independent text entries and

00:05:23.412 --> 00:05:26.660
handle each one inside its own loop iteration.

00:05:39.885 --> 00:05:42.783
These inputs represent a request for three

00:05:42.783 --> 00:05:45.473
items, a word, and a negative quantity.

00:05:45.473 --> 00:05:48.371
They let us observe acceptance, conversion

00:05:48.371 --> 00:05:51.130
failure, and rule failure in one report.

00:05:51.130 --> 00:05:54.235
The try block belongs to a single request, so

00:05:54.235 --> 00:05:57.339
handling that request's failure can leave the

00:05:57.339 --> 00:05:59.685
next request available to process.

00:05:59.721 --> 00:06:02.769
Let's print a returned quantity when reading

00:06:02.769 --> 00:06:03.393
succeeds.

00:06:03.393 --> 00:06:06.302
If the reader throws our checked exception

00:06:06.302 --> 00:06:09.627
instead, we'll print its application message and

00:06:09.627 --> 00:06:12.121
whether it retains an earlier cause.

00:06:43.032 --> 00:06:45.984
Java must finish the read call before it can

00:06:45.984 --> 00:06:46.991
print Quantity.

00:06:46.991 --> 00:06:50.211
If read throws, that success line is skipped and

00:06:50.211 --> 00:06:52.291
the handler prints the problem.

00:06:52.291 --> 00:06:54.774
GetMessage retrieves our explanation.

00:06:54.774 --> 00:06:57.726
GetCause retrieves the earlier exception, or

00:06:57.726 --> 00:07:00.813
null; comparing it with null produces the true

00:07:00.813 --> 00:07:01.886
or false status.

00:07:01.886 --> 00:07:05.107
Let's click Run and examine all three responses.

00:07:10.359 --> 00:07:13.187
The first entry prints Quantity: three because

00:07:13.187 --> 00:07:15.953
conversion succeeds and three is nonnegative.

00:07:15.953 --> 00:07:18.903
The word prints our whole-number message and Has

00:07:18.903 --> 00:07:21.608
cause: true, because the original conversion

00:07:21.608 --> 00:07:23.022
exception was retained.

00:07:23.022 --> 00:07:25.542
Negative one prints the negative-quantity

00:07:25.542 --> 00:07:27.325
message and Has cause: false.

00:07:27.325 --> 00:07:30.275
It was already a valid integer, so its rejection

00:07:30.275 --> 00:07:32.734
has no earlier parsing failure attached.

00:07:32.795 --> 00:07:36.026
We now have evidence for both kinds of failure,

00:07:36.026 --> 00:07:38.707
and neither is confused with a returned

00:07:38.707 --> 00:07:39.326
quantity.

00:07:39.326 --> 00:07:42.557
Let's change only the input array to a boundary

00:07:42.557 --> 00:07:45.651
value, a different word, and another negative

00:07:45.651 --> 00:07:46.064
value.

00:07:46.064 --> 00:07:49.020
The reader and its rule will stay the same.

00:07:54.509 --> 00:07:57.635
Before running, predict the complete report for

00:07:57.635 --> 00:08:00.229
zero, the word many, and negative four.

00:08:00.229 --> 00:08:03.023
Which entries return a quantity, and which

00:08:03.023 --> 00:08:05.218
failures retain an earlier cause?

00:08:05.218 --> 00:08:08.211
Use the two checks we just traced: conversion

00:08:08.211 --> 00:08:10.406
first, then the nonnegative rule.

00:08:10.406 --> 00:08:12.734
Pause here to make your prediction.

00:08:20.827 --> 00:08:24.201
Let's click Run and compare the actual responses

00:08:24.201 --> 00:08:25.677
with your prediction.

00:08:30.711 --> 00:08:32.476
Zero prints Quantity: zero.

00:08:32.476 --> 00:08:35.223
The rule rejects values below zero, so the

00:08:35.223 --> 00:08:36.988
boundary value is accepted.

00:08:36.988 --> 00:08:39.473
Many cannot convert to an integer; its

00:08:39.473 --> 00:08:42.480
application exception keeps the earlier cause.

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:45.488
Negative four converts, then fails validation,

00:08:45.488 --> 00:08:46.992
so its cause is absent.

00:08:46.992 --> 00:08:49.673
The different messages and cause statuses

00:08:49.673 --> 00:08:52.811
explain different paths through the same method.

00:08:52.865 --> 00:08:55.366
We built a quantity reader that returns

00:08:55.366 --> 00:08:58.443
acceptable values and gives callers a consistent

00:08:58.443 --> 00:08:59.918
way to handle failures.

00:08:59.918 --> 00:09:02.739
You used throw to raise a particular object,

00:09:02.739 --> 00:09:05.624
throws to declare a possible checked failure,

00:09:05.624 --> 00:09:08.510
and a cause to preserve an earlier exception.

00:09:08.510 --> 00:09:11.010
Those distinctions help callers respond

00:09:11.010 --> 00:09:13.896
appropriately while keeping useful diagnostic

00:09:13.896 --> 00:09:14.665
information.

00:09:14.721 --> 00:09:18.027
For a transfer question, imagine the supply desk

00:09:18.027 --> 00:09:21.195
changes its rule to require at least one item.

00:09:21.195 --> 00:09:24.295
Which validation condition should change, and

00:09:24.295 --> 00:09:27.050
what should the caller observe for zero?

00:09:27.050 --> 00:09:30.218
Keep conversion and validation separate as you

00:09:30.218 --> 00:09:31.596
explain your answer.
